course: dictionary
lesson: 6449
title: Measuring the very tiny
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some things are far too small to see, even with a magnifying glass. To measure them we need a very small unit of length. A nanometre is one billionth of a metre, written 0.000000001 m and shortened to nm. «A computer chip has billions of tiny switches printed layer by layer with light onto very pure silicon; the newest chips are described in nanometres, billionths of a metre.» The words billionths of a metre tell you the size: the parts of the chip are measured in tiny pieces of a metre.
explain: Think of a metre as a long ruler. Cut it into one billion equal pieces, and each piece is one nanometre. A millimetre is one thousandth of a metre, so it holds one million nanometres. A nanometre is far smaller than a millimetre, so use the big-to-small clue: the smaller the unit, the more of it fits in a metre.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «the newest chips are described in tiny pieces of a metre, one billionth each» still make sense? Yes, so you have understood the word.
fact: A nanometre is a unit of length, written nm.
fact: One metre is 1,000,000,000 nm (one billion nanometres).
fact: One millimetre is 1,000,000 nm (one million nanometres).
fact: Atoms, molecules, viruses and the wavelength of light are measured in nanometres.
fact: The prefix nano- means one billionth.
exercise: What is one nanometre? | One billion metres | *One billionth of a metre | One thousandth of a metre | One millionth of a metre
exercise: How many nanometres make one metre? | 1,000 | 1,000,000,000,000 | *1,000,000,000 | 1,000,000
exercise: How many nanometres make one millimetre? | 100 | 1,000,000,000 | *1,000,000 | 1,000
test: Which is the smallest length? | A kilometre | *A nanometre | A millimetre | A metre
test: Nadia says a nanometre is bigger than a metre. Is she right? | No, it is about the same as a metre | Yes, because nano means big | *No, it is one billionth of a metre, far smaller than a metre
exam: A chip part is 10 nm wide. How many such parts sit side by side across 100 nm? | 100 | 1,000 | *10 | 1
